{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 2075 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "8c33b0298f7db66f1e0ff4fcce224cbe21f5a148d0ce122dbf0d4674d5f9e34f" :edb/db-before-t 2074 :edb/db-after-t 2075 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535387 50 #inst "2026-10-08T23:15:50.430Z" 13194139535387 true] [17592186328390 1100 "Which of the following integrals evaluates to zero due to symmetry?\n\n- ${\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\sqrt[3]{x}\\,\\text{d}x$\n- ${\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x$\n- ${\\int }_{-2}^{4}\\frac{7{x}^{3}}{1+2{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x$" 13194139535387 false] [17592186328390 1100 "Which of the following integrals has an entire integrand that is odd about zero and opposite integration limits, so the standard odd-integrand cancellation rule applies directly?\n\n- ${\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\sqrt[3]{x}\\,\\text{d}x$\n- ${\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x$\n- ${\\int }_{-2}^{4}\\frac{7{x}^{3}}{1+2{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x$" 13194139535387 true] [17592186328390 1145 "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nSo, to determine which integrals evaluate to $0$ due to symmetry, we have to determine which integrands are odd functions.\n\n- The function $f(x)=\\sqrt[3]{x}$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =\\sqrt[3]{-x} \\\\  & =-\\sqrt[3]{x} \\\\  & =-f(x)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\sqrt[3]{x}\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\n- The function $g(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ is *not* an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(-x) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-x)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-x) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & \\ne -g(x)\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The function $h(x)=\\frac{7{x}^{3}}{1+2{x}^{4}}$ is an odd function, but integral III is not of the form ${\\int }_{-a}^{a}h(x)\\,\\text{d}x$ since the upper and lower limits are not opposites. Therefore, we cannot use symmetry to simplify the integral.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139535387 false] [17592186328390 1145 "The criterion in this question is the direct odd-integrand cancellation rule: if the entire integrand is odd about zero and is integrable on $[-a,a]$, then its integral is zero.\n\nI satisfies that criterion. Its integrand $\\sqrt[3]{x}$ is odd and continuous on $[-1,1]$, so $\\int_{-1}^{1}\\sqrt[3]{x}\\,\\mathrm{d}x=0$.\n\nII does not satisfy the criterion because $\\sin x+\\cos x$ is not odd about zero: its sine term is odd but its cosine term is even. Nevertheless, the integral in II also has value zero. Both sine and cosine integrate to zero over the full period $[-\\pi,\\pi]$:\n\n$$\\int_{-\\pi}^{\\pi}(\\sin x+\\cos x)\\,\\mathrm{d}x=[-\\cos x+\\sin x]_{-\\pi}^{\\pi}=1-1=0.$$\n\nFailing the odd-integrand criterion does not imply a nonzero integral; II's cancellation comes from its full-period structure.\n\nIII has an odd integrand, but the endpoints $-2$ and $4$ are not opposites. It therefore does not satisfy the direct criterion. Its value is\n\n$$\\int_{-2}^{4}\\frac{7x^3}{1+2x^4}\\,\\mathrm{d}x=\\frac78[\\ln(1+2x^4)]_{-2}^{4}=\\frac78\\ln\\frac{513}{33}\\ne0.$$\n\nThus “I only” is correct for the stated direct odd-integrand criterion. If the question asked which integrals have value zero by any method, the answer would instead be I and II." 13194139535387 true]] :edb/tempids {}}
